Tags are labels on a resource, like `team = "team-sales"` or `owner = "grp-sales-dev-owners"`. Changing them is a real change, and it does show up in the plan. That part is right.

What it misses is *how* Terraform makes the change. Azure lets you edit tags on a running workspace. So Terraform updates the workspace **in place**. That means it keeps the same workspace and just edits it. The plan marks this with `~`, not `-/+`:

```
~ resource "azurerm_databricks_workspace" "this" {
  ~ tags = {
      + "owner" = "grp-sales-dev-owners"
    }
```

Nothing is destroyed. The URL stays the same. Clusters, notebooks and jobs inside the workspace are untouched. A tag change in a Spacelift plan for DEV, UAT or PROD is usually safe to approve.

Changing `managed_resource_group_name` is the better answer because Azure can't rename that resource group on an existing workspace, so Terraform must destroy the workspace and build a new one, which shows as `-/+` with `# forces replacement`.
